Latency vs Throughput 
System design (HLD)
The one-line idea
	•	Latency = how long ONE request takes. (Speed of a single task)
	•	Throughput = how many requests we finish per second. (Volume of work)
Simple analogy: a highway
	•	Latency = time for one car to go from A to B (say 30 mins).
	•	Throughput = how many cars pass a point per hour.
	•	Adding more lanes increases throughput. It does NOT make a single car faster.
	•	Raising the speed limit reduces latency. It does not add capacity.
They are related, but they are not the same thing.
Units to remember
	•	Latency: milliseconds (ms). Always talk in p50, p95, p99, not averages. Averages hide pain.
	•	Throughput: requests per second (RPS/QPS), or MB/s.
How they connect (Little's Law)
Concurrent requests = Throughput × Latency
Example: 1,000 RPS at 200 ms latency means about 200 requests in flight at once. If latency rises, the same traffic needs more threads and connections. This is why a slow dependency can bring down a whole system.
Why it is a trade-off
In HLD, improving one often hurts the other.
