Real examples
	•	Kafka producer: linger.ms and batch size. Larger batches give higher throughput but add a few ms of latency. Set linger to 0 for low latency.
	•	Payment API: needs low latency. The user is waiting, so every ms matters.
	•	Nightly reports / data pipelines: need high throughput. Nobody cares if one record takes 2 seconds, as long as we process 10 million records in an hour.
	•	Search or feed: both matter. We cache and precompute (fan-out on write) to keep reads fast at scale.

Ask first: "Who is waiting, a human or a machine?"
	•	Human waiting (checkout, login, search): optimize latency, set a p99 target.
	•	Machine waiting (ETL, analytics, email sending): optimize throughput, use batching and queues.
	•	Often we need both, so split the paths:
	•	Keep the sync path thin and fast.
	•	Push heavy work to async workers for throughput.
Common mistakes
	•	Scaling out servers to fix a latency problem. More machines do not speed up one request.
	•	Reporting average latency. Always check p99.
	•	Running systems at 100% capacity. Keep headroom (around 60-70%), because queueing delay explodes near the limit.
	•	Batching everywhere without asking whether users are waiting.
Interview-ready summary
"Latency is the time per request. Throughput is the requests per second. I pick based on who is waiting. Humans need low latency, background systems need high throughput. I use caching and thin sync paths for latency, and batching, queues, and horizontal scaling for throughput."
